From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: sunxi: h713-r: mux 0xF is io_disabled

Every R_PIO pin comes out of reset with its mux field at 0xF, I/O disabled
(input and output buffers off). The table only lists the functions a
consumer might want, so once anything muxes a pin away, pinctrl has no way
to put it back as it was found: a consumer's "sleep" state can at best be
gpio_in, which leaves the input buffer on.

Measured on the bench board on 2026-10-15: before the HDMI DDC helper
claimed PL10-PL15, their fields read 0xffffff (all 0xF), and the unclaimed
PL8 reads 0xF as well. The HDMI signal owner needs to return those pins
exactly, so it needs a function it can select.

Allwinner's own BSP names this function "io_disabled". Add it at 0xF for
every R_PIO pin. It is only an additional selectable entry: nothing changes
unless a consumer's pinctrl state asks for it. The main PIO has the same
gap and is left for a separate patch.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sun50i-h713-r.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sun50i-h713-r.c
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@
 		  SUNXI_FUNCTION(0x1, "gpio_out"),
 		  SUNXI_FUNCTION(0x2, "s_rsb"),		/* SCK */
 		  SUNXI_FUNCTION(0x3, "s_i2c"),		/* SCK */
+		  SUNXI_FUNCTION(0xf, "io_disabled"),
 		  SUNXI_FUNCTION_IRQ_BANK(0xe, 0, 0)),	/* PL_EINT0 */
 	/* PL1: s_rsb/s_i2c data */
 	SUNXI_PIN(SUNXI_PINCTRL_PIN(L, 1),
@@ -58,30 +59,35 @@
